course: dictionary
lesson: 8552
title: Spotting a scammer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A scammer is a person who tricks people to steal their money or personal details. When you meet this word, read the words around it for clues. «If a number you do not know calls or messages you, let a parent answer or ignore it, because unknown callers can be scammers.» The clue is "unknown callers": a scammer can be someone you have never met.
explain: «Companies never ask for gift card codes; scammers like them because the money cannot be traced or returned.» The second half of the sentence tells you why. Scammers want money they cannot get back. Ask yourself what the person wants from you, and that helps you spot the trick.
explain: A boy in Phoenix gets a call from an unknown number asking «Is your mum home?» He lets his dad answer. That was the safe choice, because he did not give anything away to a stranger and an adult took over.
fact: A scammer is a person who tricks people to steal their money or personal details.
fact: «Scammers» often use fake messages, phone calls, websites or prizes that sound too good to be true.
fact: Warning signs include hurry, secrecy, prizes, and requests for passwords or money.
fact: Companies never ask for gift card codes, so anyone who asks you to pay with one is almost certainly a scammer.
fact: «Scammers have learned to use AI as well», so do not trust a message just because it looks or sounds real.
fact: If you think you have met a scammer, do not reply, and tell a trusted adult. That is how you report a scammer.
exercise: What is a scammer? | A person who sells ice cream in the park | *A person who tricks people to steal their money or personal details | A teacher who marks spelling tests
exercise: «Anyone who asks you to pay them in gift cards is almost certainly a scammer.» Which is a warning sign? | Your dad makes you a cup of tea | *Someone asks you to pay them in gift cards | A friend invites you to the park
exercise: Why do scammers like gift card codes? | Because gift cards are heavy to carry | *Because the money cannot be traced or returned | Because companies give them away for free
test: Ella gets a message saying «You have won a prize! Reply with your bank password now.» What should she do? | Send the password, it sounds fun | Reply quickly before the prize goes | *Do not reply, and tell a trusted adult
test: Which phrase means you should tell someone about a scammer? | Share a password with a scammer | *Report a scammer | Fall for a scammer
exam: What should you do if an unknown number asks you for a gift card code? | *Hang up, and tell a parent or trusted adult | Send them some money to be safe | Read the code out so they stop calling | Ask them for a prize first
